{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261027T232857Z-a820d745", "ts": "2026-10-27T23:32:25Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.0-dev.2", "branch": "fix/1092-chunk-state-table"}, "body": {"base_commit": "eb4de60ea330e0d9cfc8e1a40665936e76b2d2ad", "base_tree": "d28058350bc526e7fcfae56e34065884436ebebd", "head_tree": "9f4651a7c07f4c38801a910df1c07666113dc821", "head_commit": "39dcde207cbd3c380d27a56908365b55b8a0e88f", "dispatch_commit": "39dcde207cbd3c380d27a56908365b55b8a0e88f", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/pr/review-protocol.md", "tests/test_chunk_state_table.py", "tests/test_plan_lifecycle.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_uncertified_chunks_close.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/pr/review-protocol.md", "tests/test_chunk_state_table.py", "tests/test_plan_lifecycle.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_uncertified_chunks_close.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "blocking", "title": "Saved test evidence records 2 failing tests", "recommendation": "`prawduct-hook test-status` (this repo's own binary, CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT unset) exits 1: `stale: 2 test(s) failing in saved evidence: tests.test_aggregate_stats.TestCollectorFetch::test_an_index_that_is_not_an_index_exits_1[...]` and `tests.test_backlog_title_enforcement::test_file_refuses_each_failing_rule[...title-too-long-backlog...]`. Neither file is in this diff, and both parametrize ids are huge generated strings (a long run of `[`, and a repeated clause), so this may be a flake or an id artifact rather than a regression. Under the protocol, failures in the evidence are BLOCKING whoever caused them. Fix: run the full suite on HEAD and record it. A green run clears this. If either test fails again, fix it or record it as flaky. Scope: instance.", "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "A span-certified chunk sharing a Deliverables path cannot be kept open: --chunks no longer bounds it and retraction is per-fact", "recommendation": "Why it broke: `_Spans.carried` credits every chunk whose Deliverables a commit in the fact's interval touches (`_deliverable_chunks`). When two chunks list the same path, the commit that built one also 'builds' the other. The docstring and the plan's Goals both say 'an unbuilt chunk has no commit, so no span certifies it', and that is false here. Before this change the same overlap only reached the proposal, which the builder saw on stderr and could narrow with `--chunks`. Now a span certifies the chunk whatever `--chunks` says (SKILL.md step 4 and the budget comment admit as much). The only remaining fix is `plan retract`, and `withdrawn()` matches on (fact_id, chunk). So the next review whose interval holds that commit certifies the chunk again, and the boundary `cumulative` (merge-base..HEAD) always does. The plan then reads finished, and `archive-plan completed` passes, with the chunk unbuilt. This is not rare: a scratch query of `plan_status.deliverable_paths` over the 172 live and archived plans in this repo found 46 where two chunks list the exact same Deliverables path. No test pins it either: the fixtures give every chunk a disjoint `src/cNN.py`, and X3 retracts against a single fact. Scope: class (span attribution by a path several chunks share). Fix options: (a) when a span touches a path several open chunks share, credit none of them by path alone (subject only), or (b) make a span retraction hold against every span fact over the same commit, keyed by commit and chunk. Add an overlap row and a two-fact retraction row to the case table, and correct the docstring claim.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "tests/test_chunk_state_table.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "note", "title": "First-parent rule accepts a fork-point anchor that a stacked branch's merge brought onto the base", "recommendation": "`on_first_parent` treats 'off the base's first-parent history' as 'brought in by this plan's merge'. Take branch B, forked from branch A's HEAD while A was still unmerged. B's first review is a dirty-tree review dispatched before B has commits, so its anchor and its base_commit are A's HEAD. A then merges --no-ff into develop and B is deleted unmerged. B's anchor is now on develop through the merge's second parent, so `_Reachability` accepts it and the chunks that review named read done. `plan_lifecycle._merge_evidence` would likewise report B as merged-unreleased. The docstring's 'authority fails closed' does not hold in this shape. Only the simple fork-point case (M8) is pinned. It needs a stacked, abandoned branch plus a named review before the first commit, so it is rare. Consider adding a row that pins it, or document the limit. Scope: instance.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "on_first_parent is a git-history primitive homed in plan_status, so plan_lifecycle imports the chunk-state module for it; the composed refusal policy still appears twice", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. `on_first_parent(repo_dir, anchor, base)` reads only git and nothing about chunks or plans, but it lives in plan_status.py. That gives plan_lifecycle.py a direct `plan_status` import for one git predicate. It adds no cycle, since plan_lifecycle already reaches plan_status through release_readiness, gates and plan_status. The rule built on it, 'an anchor that is its own base_commit is refused iff on_first_parent', is written separately in `_Reachability.__call__` and in `_merge_evidence`, so 'one owner' covers the predicate but not the policy. `_Reachability._on_first_parent` is a one-call wrapper that adds nothing. Consider moving the predicate to gitstate (where the other git ancestry helpers live), or exporting the composed `refuses_base_commit_anchor(repo, anchor, base, is_base_commit)` decision so both callers pass through it, and drop the wrapper. No behaviour change is wanted.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Span docstring and comment say chunk state and the coverage gate 'cannot disagree' about spans, but a dirty-tree fact edited before commit is a coverage edge that spans nothing", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The module docstring ('That is the span the coverage gate composes over, so the two instruments cannot disagree about it') and the inline comment in chunk_states ('so chunk state and the coverage gate agree on which facts span anything') say more than the code does. `coverage_algebra.review_edges` gates on tree validity and reviewed-set completeness. `_Spans.carried` then walks commits and, for a dirty-tree fact, counts the uncommitted part only once it has been committed verbatim (row C11). So an edge the gate composes can certify no chunk. The shared part is necessary eligibility (only edges may span), not agreement. Make the claim relational ('only a fact the coverage algebra accepts as an edge may span') or delete the 'cannot disagree' clause. This is prose that nothing reads, so no fix commit is owed beyond that.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Test module docstring cites the case table by a live plan path that archiving will dangle", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. tests/test_chunk_state_table.py says it is 'one test per row' of `.prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-state-table.md` '## Case table'. Archiving the plan moves that file into archive/, so the path dangles; Goal 4 says a plan resolves by scope, not path. Cite the plan by scope (`chunk-state-table`), or treat the row ids in the test names as the durable index and drop the path.", "files": ["tests/test_chunk_state_table.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Reflowed comment in build_fact_body left a 121-column line and a doubled 'beside'", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. critic_consolidate.py around line 5121 reads '`plan_status.chunk_states` certifies from, beside the span \u2014 with the code-derived proposal beside them, so a', which is 121 columns while the comment around it wraps near 80. Rewrap it when the file is next touched. No edit is owed for this round.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Framework-Specific Checks: Generality, Instruction Clarity, Cumulative Health, Pipeline Coverage pass for the instruction edits", "recommendation": "Scope: none. I checked the edits to planning.md 'Chunk state', building.md 'Critic review', critic SKILL.md step 4 and pr/review-protocol.md 'Chunk progress'. They state the name-or-span rule generally, with no product-specific enumeration. They agree with one another and with plan_status: '--chunks names chunks this review certifies; its span may certify more' and 'omit the flag to name exactly the proposal'. They introduce no new concern needing registry coverage. I searched for the retired phrasing ('certified no chunk', 'certifies only the proposal', 'the chunks it names', 'what the fact certifies') across plugin/, documentation/ and the live artifacts. It survives only in history-bearing archived or sibling plans, which are exempt. One gap: planning.md leaves out two span preconditions (the fact must be a fully reviewed coverage edge, and a dirty-tree part counts only once committed verbatim). Both fail closed, so no reader takes a wrong action because of it.", "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "rule-unenforced: an agent-authored extension of owner ruling D1 lands with no owner confirmation on the record", "recommendation": "Core learning 'A governance change cannot supply its own authority: when an agent amends a binding norm mid-build, land the owner's confirmation somewhere the amendment isn't'. data-model.md's Direction ruling D1 (owner, 2026-10-24) gains 'Extended 2026-10-27', which cites only this plan's Goals and 'the coordinator's design decision for #1082'. The plan says the owner's confirmation of option 1 'lives with the coordinator, not here'. The owner asked for #1079/#1082 to land in 3.8.0, which is not the same as choosing option 1 (span-derived) over option 2. planning.md's 'Chunk state' and plan_status.chunk_states implement the extension, so right now the amendment is its own only witness. Instance remedy: record the owner's confirm-or-veto of option 1 somewhere the amendment isn't (a decision fact, or a comment on #1082 that the extension cites). The plan's 'Not settled by the goals' forks (span only through a coverage edge, dirty-tree verbatim commit, retract-while-spanning) can be bulk-confirmed in the same step. Class remedy, because nothing deterministic owns this rule (record_lint CHECKS has no norm-amendment check; the PR protocol's 'Norm amendments carry their decision' is reviewer judgment). Add a record_lint check modelled on learnings-core-raise-unapproved: flag added lines inside a governed_by artifact's '## Direction' block unless they cite a decision or owner-confirmation fact. Scope: class.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-state-table.md"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Goal 5 mid-build decisions, for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none. The plan records these decisions and what settled each. (1) Option 1, span-derived done, settled by the coordinator: the coverage gate already composes by span. (2) A 'built commit' is a commit in the FACT's own interval, not built_report's whole-branch walk, settled by rows C14 and C6. (3) A span certifies only through a coverage_algebra.review_edges edge, so the two instruments agree. (4) A dirty-tree span's uncommitted part counts only once committed verbatim (C10, C11). (5) Fast-forward with anchor = base_commit (M5) and squash (M6) stay open, failing closed. (6) First-parent refusal applies only to the anchor == base_commit case (M4 preserved). (7) Naming answers before span, and a span-done carries a reason. (8) plan retract withdraws a span only while it certifies. (9) D1 gets a dated extension note rather than a rewrite. (10) on_first_parent is one owner shared with plan_lifecycle. Alignment pass: 'Proceed, citing #1092, #1079 and #1082'. The only miss is (1)/(9)'s owner confirmation, covered by the finding above. Every capability traces to #1079/#1082/#1092, and each new helper (holder, _named_ids, _deliverable_chunks, _log_commits, on_first_parent) has a caller.", "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "uncertified_clause states a cause it never derived: 'neither its proposal nor its interval found work'", "recommendation": "_uncertified_open_chunks returns every chunk still open after a whole-plan review that named none. A chunk can be open there because the fact still has an unresolved blocker, is not a coverage edge (C8), or because the span's git log failed (F2/F3). In those cases its interval may well carry the chunk's commit, yet the appended clause asserts 'no commit subject in it names them and no Deliverables path was touched'. Remedy: drop the causal parenthetical, or make it relational ('no review certifies them yet'). Do not reword the narration. The remedy it offers (pass --chunks) still works, so the harm is a wrong diagnosis rather than a wrong action. Scope: instance.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "A span that fails to certify leaves no reason on the open chunk", "recommendation": "The named path explains a refusal in ChunkState.reason ('its commit is not on ...', the blocker). The span loop skips without a note for every refusal: a blocked fact, a non-edge, an anchor not reachable, a failed interval log, or an unknown holder. Rows F2-F4 and C8 assert only 'open'. Someone debugging 'why is chunk 03 open after a clean review' gets reason None. This fails safe and the remedy is the same either way, so it is a NOTE. If you want it diagnosable, note() the first span refusal per chunk the way the named path does. Scope: instance.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Per-Stop span cost grows with the plan's edge review facts while any chunk is open", "recommendation": "The NF1 docstring states the per-fact cost honestly (one git log per interval, plus a git show per change-fragment path, memoized per call) and G1/G2 pin it. Mid-plan, though, an unbuilt chunk is always open, so the early break never fires. Every Stop then logs every edge review fact's interval in scope, and that set only grows over a plan's life. Commits and trees are immutable, so a cross-call memo keyed by (base, head|dispatch, head_tree) would make this a one-time cost. No edit is owed until a Stop-latency measurement says it matters. Scope: instance.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: #1079, #1082, #1092", "recommendation": "Backlog items appear resolved: brookstalley/prawduct#1079 'governance: dirty-tree chunk review reads open after its plan merges', #1082 'plan status: a chunk covered by an unnamed review stays open', and #1092 'governance: chunk state derived from review facts keeps regressing'. All three are still open in the cache, and the change record lists them in closes:. Verify each, then call /prawduct:backlog update <id> status=shipped closed-by=scope:chunk-state-table when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says. Scope: none.", "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "C-B3: open item in the touched area \u2014 #672 (coverage composes by tree but its gates key on identity)", "recommendation": "Running 'affecting' over plan_status.py, plan_lifecycle.py, critic_consolidate.py and planning.md returns #672 (and #928, unrelated) besides the closed pair. This change makes chunk state compose by the coverage algebra's span, which is #672's subject. Assess whether #672's scope moved and update it. Out-of-scope #1106 and #1071 are still open, as the plan intends. Scope: none.", "fid": "R-15"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check and Records Pass: coverage", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Learnings read list ('learnings-files --for-diff'): core, authoring, gates, hook-surface, pr, release, reviews, tests. This cycle added or changed no learnings rule, so there is no added-rule pass. Rules checked against the change: hook-surface 'A docstring or comment is a claim'. _log_commits says it is 'the one reader' of chunk-to-commit commits, and it is the only git log in plan_status.py, so that holds. authoring 'Editing a norm's STATEMENT in the same commit as the code it would bless': D1 got a dated extension beneath the clause rather than a rewrite, which conforms. Core 'A governance change cannot supply its own authority' fired: see the rule-unenforced finding. Records Pass, set covered: api-contract.md, build-plan-chunk-state-table.md, data-model.md, changes/chunk-state-table.md and cross-cutting-concerns.md. Spot-checks held: the change record's claim that plan check-claims reads #1079's case via plan_lifecycle.observe, its M5/M6 consumer caveat, and api-contract's not-named refusal text. No record clears a ship, mislead-into-action or stop-the-merge bar beyond the D1 authority gap already filed.", "fid": "R-16"}], "counts": {"blocking": 1, "warning": 2, "note": 13}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 540, "scope": "chunk-state-table", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunks_proposed": ["01", "02", "03"], "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "eb4de60ea330e0d9cfc8e1a40665936e76b2d2ad", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-state-table.md", ".prawduct/cross-cutting-concerns.md", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skill